## Fix 6 — resync React's tree after composition

**Symptom:** after confirming an IME composition, the next keystroke silently
wipes the composed text, or characters land in the wrong styled run.

**Cause:** the browser wrote text React never rendered, so React's virtual tree
is stale. If a subsequent render produces a run whose text is unchanged *from
React's point of view*, React skips the DOM write and the stale browser-written
text survives.

**Fix:** keep a counter that increments on composition commit and include it in
the `key` of each styled run. A changed key remounts the runs, guaranteeing React
re-owns the DOM. This is cheap — a handful of elements — and it runs only on
composition commit, not on every keystroke.

```js
const [revision, resync] = useReducer((n) => n + 1, 0);
// ...
<span key={`a-${revision}`} className="run-a">{parts.a}</span>
```

Consider making this general rather than composition-specific: an `input`
listener that compares `el.textContent` against `value` and calls `resync()` when
they differ will also recover from any non-cancelable `beforeinput` that slips
through.

